      "s63-c6-principal",
      "Principal and Agency Cross Trades",
      "Advisers that are also broker-dealers sometimes sell securities to clients from the firm's own inventory. This is a principal transaction. It can be a conflict, because the IAR may advise a client to buy shares that benefit the firm.\n\nIn a principal transaction with a client, the adviser must:\n• Fully disclose the firm's role and describe the conflict to the client.\n• Get the client's written permission before the trade settles.\n• Send a confirmation that shows the firm acted as principal, says the client was told, and notes the client's permission.\n\nAn agency cross transaction happens when a dual adviser and broker-dealer acts for both sides of a trade. The firm advises one client to buy from or sell to another of its clients and makes the trade. To do this, the firm must:\n• Get written consent from both clients after full disclosure.\n• Send both clients a written confirmation at or before the end of each trade.\n• Disclose in writing each year the number of agency cross trades and the total commissions earned.\n\nIn an agency cross trade, the firm can charge an advisory fee to one client and a commission to the other. It cannot charge advisory fees to both.",
