      "When an adviser or IAR recommends a security the firm owns, it must tell the client. Advisers and IARs must also disclose if they are trading against their own recommendations to clients.\n\nAdvisers and IARs may not do anything indirectly, or through someone else, that would be illegal to do directly. If you ask or hire someone to do something securities law bans you from doing, you are still breaking the law.\n\nSelling away is when an agent is paid for securities work that the employing firm does not supervise. It is banned. To avoid it, an agent must tell the broker-dealer about all outside business and investment activities. If the agent wants to sell outside securities for pay, the trade can happen only if the firm approves it, supervises it, and records it on its books.\n\nAn agent must not use false or misleading information to open an account at any broker-dealer to make trades that are otherwise banned.",
